    "shift": "unknown_shift"
  },
  "question": {
    "text": "Two uniform solid spheres made of copper have radii&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> respectively. Both of them are heated to a temperature of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>70</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;and then placed in a region of ambient temperature equal to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>. What will be the ratio of the initial rates of cooling of both the spheres?"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "01.33",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>For two bodies made up of same material, rate of loss of heat will depend only on their surface area for same temperature change.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;&#160;</mo><mfrac><msub><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></mfenced></math></p><p>Ratio of initial rates of cooling<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math></p><p>As,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>dQ</mi><mi>dt</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>mc</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow><mi>dt</mi></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>dt</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>dt</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></math>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; ...[from (i)]</p><p>since, both the spheres are made up of copper,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;&#160;</mo><mfrac><msub><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#215;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#215;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>As, spheres have same densities,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>33</mn></math></p>"
  }
}